# Conventional Commits

All commit messages MUST follow Conventional Commits.

## Format

```text
type(optional-scope): subject

optional body

optional footer
```

## Types (English)

Use one of: `feat`, `fix`, `docs`, `style`, `refactor`, `test`, `chore`, `build`, `ci`.

## Scope

- English, kebab-case or a short noun.
- Recommended when the change targets a clear area. Not mandatory for a tiny chore.
- New scopes are allowed. The examples are not a closed list.

## Subject

- English, including the body.
- Type and scope stay in English.
- Imperative and concise. No trailing period required.
- Focus on why when that helps. Do not dump file lists.

## Breaking changes

Use `type(scope)!: subject` and/or a footer:

```text
BREAKING CHANGE: description
```

## Examples

```text
feat(predicates): add the exact sign fallback
fix(hull): classify coplanar points after the merge
docs(roadmap): mark P1 in progress
chore(ci): add the fmt and clippy job
```

## Agent behavior

When creating commits in this repository, draft messages in this format. Do not use a free-form subject without a type prefix. Commit only when the user asks (`.cursor/rules/git.mdc`).
